# Display Dolls!

At the annual conference of state governors held in Delhi, the all-India capital, Dr. Pattabhi Sitaramayya, himself a governor, is said to have made this complaint before President Prasad and Pandit Nehru, who presided in splendour:

> "Even matters of importance concerning a state have to be learned by us governors from the daily newspapers. The state ministers do not even inform us what they are doing or what is happening."

Pattabhi asks plaintively, "Sir, even if you do not place your actions before us for consultation, can you not at least inform us before acting?"

Under the Constitution, the governments ruling the states are answerable to their legislatures, not to the governor. They need not consult him before carrying out their work. That is why everyone has been observing that a governor's position carries no responsibility or difficulty and requires him merely to remain like a doll on display.

If one asks why democracy needs such a doll when the country is governed democratically, Delhi offers no proper reply. The governorship has increasingly become a means of giving favoured persons a "chance," and of occupying, touring, and keeping clean the mansions vacated by the British. Apart from reading a statement prepared by government secretaries once or twice a year in the name of opening the legislature, there is no other work. The remaining days are spent attending banquets, engaging in pleasant conversation, and inviting favoured people for tours.

Our household taxes pay for this life. Prakasam alone costs ten lakh rupees a year!

When asked whether this injustice is tolerable, the Congress "honourables" do not open their mouths. If anyone does, they brush it aside: "What can we do? Is this not a matter for the high command?" It is that easy to dissolve the people's tax money.

It is said that even officials at the centre do not respect these governors. Delhi State Development Minister Brahm Prakash says that officials of the Central Government treat them like mere clerks. No honour there and no respect here, yet Pattabhi and Prakasam consent to remain governors, for the lord called money! But why should the government consent to waste the people's tax money in this way? Ten lakh rupees disappears every year.

We hope that the rebuke of people like Pattabhi Sitaramayya will help today's government acquire the democratic instinct to spend in each state, on its necessary development work, the sum allocated there for these people who have no work.

Instead of complaining that governors are not granted authority and respect appropriate to their position, they could speak forcefully enough for Delhi to understand that maintaining an office called "governor" and wasting lakhs of rupees upon it is needless, unnecessary ostentation. That could be considered wisdom. Merely expressing shame-filled sorrow that "what happens in the country takes place without us governors knowing it" benefits neither the country nor themselves.

Anyone with human intelligence who examines the government's expenditure figures for the ten lakh rupees given to a governor cannot help exclaiming, "Alas, must money be wasted even like this?"

Even the cattle displayed to hunt for votes would shed tears on hearing the sum spent on a governor. Yet to the "honourables" and Kamaraj, all this continues to be a very small matter!

(Publication line damaged in OCR, 1956)
